{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher tumor mutational burden is associated with shorter overall survival in TCGA breast cancer patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether patients with higher somatic mutation counts have reduced overall survival compared to those with lower mutation counts.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. For each bcr_patient_barcode, count total mutations to define tumor mutational burden (TMB). 3. Merge TMB with clinical data (days_to_death, days_to_last_followup, vital_status) by barcode. 4. Define high‐TMB vs low‐TMB groups (e.g., top vs bottom quartile). 5. Perform Kaplan–Meier survival analysis comparing groups, and a log‐rank test. 6. Optionally fit a Cox proportional hazards model including TMB (continuous) and adjust for age and stage. 7. Visualize survival curves and forest plot of hazard ratios.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of TMB per patient and group assignment. 2. Kaplan–Meier survival curves for TMB groups. 3. Log‐rank test p‐value and, if performed, Cox model hazard ratio with 95% CI. 4. Forest plot of Cox model coefficients."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"ESR1 (ER‐alpha) gene expression quantitatively distinguishes clinical ER‐positive from ER‐negative breast tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether expression levels of the ESR1 gene predict immunohistochemistry‐determined ER status.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Identify the Ensembl ID for ESR1 in the expression matrix. 3. Filter patients to those with non‐missing er_status and matching expression sample. 4. Extract ESR1 expression values and group by er_status (Positive vs Negative). 5. Compare distributions using a two‐sample t‐test or Wilcoxon test. 6. Generate a receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve of ESR1 expression predicting ER status and compute AUC. 7. Plot boxplots of ESR1 expression by ER status with p‐value annotation.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of ESR1 expression by ER status. 2. Test statistic and p‐value for group comparison. 3. ROC curve and AUC. 4. Boxplot of expression by ER status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"TP53‐mutant breast tumors exhibit elevated expression of key immune markers (CD8A, CD3D, CD274) relative to TP53 wild‐type tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether TP53 mutation status is associated with differences in expression of canonical immune‐related genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv, cleaned_gene_expression.csv, cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Flag each patient as TP53‐mutant if they harbor ≥1 TP53 mutation. 3. Match mutation flags to expression samples. 4. Extract expression of CD8A, CD3D, and CD274 (PD‐L1) for TP53‐mutant and wild‐type groups. 5. Compare each gene’s expression between groups using a t‐test or Mann–Whitney test. 6. Adjust the three p‐values via Benjamini–Hochberg. 7. Create boxplots for each marker showing group differences.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of mean expression and group counts for each marker. 2. Test statistics, raw p‐values, and FDR‐adjusted p‐values. 3. Boxplots of marker expression by TP53 status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients harboring at least one HIGH‐impact somatic mutation are over‐represented in advanced pathologic stages (III/IV) compared to early stages (I/II).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate enrichment of high‐impact mutation carriers in advanced vs early pathologic stages.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. For each patient, flag presence of any mutation with IMPACT='HIGH'. 3. Map pathologic_stage into two groups: Early (Stage I or II) vs Advanced (Stage III or IV). 4. Build a 2×2 contingency table of high‐impact carrier vs non‐carrier by stage group. 5. Perform Fisher’s exact test to assess enrichment and compute odds ratio and 95% CI. 6. Plot bar chart showing proportions of high‐impact carriers in each stage group.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table of carriers vs non‐carriers by stage. 2. Fisher’s test p‐value, odds ratio, and 95% CI. 3. Bar chart of carrier proportions by stage group."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Breast tumors from patients who received neoadjuvant therapy exhibit distinct gene expression profiles compared to those who did not receive neoadjuvant therapy.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Identify differentially expressed genes between neoadjuvant treatment recipients and non‐recipients.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv. 2. Filter patients to non‐missing neoadjuvant_treatment (‘Yes’ vs ‘No’) and matching expression samples. 3. Split expression matrix into two groups by treatment. 4. For each gene, perform a two‐sample test (e.g., limma or Welch t‐test) comparing expression between groups. 5. Adjust p‐values with Benjamini–Hochberg. 6. Report genes with FDR<0.05 along with log₂ fold‐changes, raw p‐values, and FDR. 7. Generate volcano plot of –log10(p‐value) vs log₂ fold‐change.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Differential expression results table (gene ID, log₂FC, p‐value, FDR). 2. Counts of significantly up‐ and down‐regulated genes. 3. Volcano plot highlighting significant genes."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumor mutational burden increases with patient age at diagnosis.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between somatic mutation count and age at diagnosis.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Compute mutation count per patient as TMB. 3. Merge TMB with age_at_diagnosis by barcode. 4. Compute Spearman correlation coefficient and p‐value between age and TMB. 5. Create a scatter plot of age vs TMB with a fitted trend line. 6. Optionally stratify by menopausal status and repeat correlation.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Spearman correlation coefficient and p‐value. 2. Scatter plot with regression line. 3. Table of TMB and age for each patient."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"History of prior malignancy is associated with reduced progression‐free survival (longer time to new tumor event).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether patients with a history of prior malignancy experience different time to new tumor event compared to those without.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Filter to patients with non‐missing prior_malignancy and days_to_new_tumor. 3. Group patients by prior_malignancy ('Yes' vs 'No'). 4. Perform Kaplan–Meier analysis of time to new tumor event (censoring those with no event) and log‐rank test. 5. Plot progression‐free survival curves for the two groups.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of event counts and median times by prior malignancy status. 2. Kaplan–Meier curves of progression‐free survival. 3. Log‐rank test p‐value."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"ERBB2 (HER2) gene expression levels correlate with clinical HER2 IHC score (0, 1+, 2+, 3+).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between ERBB2 expression and HER2 IHC scoring.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_gene_expression.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv. 2. Identify the Ensembl ID for ERBB2 in the expression matrix. 3. Filter to patients with non‐missing her2_ihc_score and matching expression samples. 4. Map her2_ihc_score categories (0, 1+, 2+, 3+) to an ordinal factor. 5. Compare ERBB2 expression across score groups using ANOVA or Kruskal–Wallis test. 6. Perform post‐hoc pairwise comparisons if overall test is significant. 7. Generate boxplots of ERBB2 expression by IHC score.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics of ERBB2 expression by score. 2. ANOVA/Kruskal–Wallis test statistic and p‐value. 3. Post‐hoc comparison p‐values. 4. Boxplot of expression by HER2 IHC score."  
    }  
  }  
]}
